I ran MaNGOS and ArcEmu servers a decade ago, and a World of Warcraft emulator
is still one of the better distributed-systems textbooks lying around in public
— mostly because so little of it is distributed.

It is also the workload I know well enough to test a cluster honestly. Split it
and almost everything is boring stateless plumbing that belongs in
[the k3s platform](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/). Exactly one process does not, and
it is the one that is the game.

These projects exist to study game server architecture and network protocol
design, and that is the only use discussed here. If you run anything, its
legality is yours to answer for.

::: note
**Part 2 of 2 of [Where to start with
self-hosting](/blog/running-things-on-your-own-hardware/).** Previous: [K3s
starter kit on Hetzner cloud](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/)
:::

## The three pieces

The same three in every project I looked at — TrinityCore, AzerothCore, CMaNGOS,
MaNGOS, AscEmu, all of which are still alive, which surprised me.

- **An authentication daemon.** `authserver`, `realmd`, `logonserver`. It
  answers the login handshake and hands the client a list of realms.
- **A world daemon.** `worldserver`, `mangosd`. This is the game: maps,
  creatures, spells, movement, loot.
- **A relational database**, split across auth, characters and world content.

The join between the first two is a table, not an API. TrinityCore's auth
database has a `realmlist` row per realm carrying `address` and `port`
([realmlist](https://github.com/TrinityCore/tc-wiki/blob/main/database/335/auth/realmlist.md)),
documented as the world server's public address. The client authenticates,
receives that address, and opens a raw TCP connection straight to the world
daemon.

I had assumed the client talks to a front door that routes. It does not. **A
protocol that publishes its own endpoint has already made your topology decision
for you** — whatever you put in front has to be a transparent TCP path, not
something you rewrite at an ingress.

The commercial article was a genuine distributed system: the CMaNGOS wiki
sketches separate nodes for Login, Connection Manager, Realm, Map, a Battlegroup
server and several instance servers, interconnected by messaging queues
([Threading model](https://github.com/cmangos/issues/wiki/Threading-model)). The
open-source emulators collapse Realm, Map and instances into one process, and
that single decision is the whole subject of this post.

## Why the world server is not a pod

TrinityCore's map manager walks every live map once per tick and either updates
it inline or hands it to a worker pool, then waits for all of them:

{title="src/server/game/Maps/MapManager.cpp"}
```cpp
if (m_updater.activated())
    m_updater.schedule_update(*iter->second, uint32(i_timer.GetCurrent()));
else
    iter->second->Update(uint32(i_timer.GetCurrent()));
...
if (m_updater.activated())
    m_updater.wait();
```

And the unit of work handed to a worker is one whole map. Two things follow.
Parallelism exists **between** maps and instances, never within one — a
continent is a single `Map` object and its update is a single task on a single
thread. And `m_updater.wait()` is a barrier every tick, so the tick costs
whatever the slowest single map costs, no matter how many threads are idle
behind it.

The shipped defaults agree: TrinityCore ships `MapUpdate.Threads = 1`, CMaNGOS
ships 3, and both ship `Network.Threads = 1` with the note "Recommended 1 thread
per 1000 connections". That is the *design*, established from source and
configuration — not a profile of a running server. The community's crowded
capital city pinning one core is consistent with this code. Consistent is not
measured.

I went in assuming a modern core with a thread pool would scale across cores, and
that the answer to a busy realm was a bigger machine. **A tick bounded by one
thread is bounded by clock, not by core count** — and the fastest single thread
you can buy lives in a desktop socket, not a server one. PassMark puts the Ryzen
9 9950X at 4,728 single-thread against the 192-core EPYC 9965 at 3,176.

Now put that process in a cluster, and the Kubernetes documentation describes
what you just agreed to:

> By default, the kubelet uses CFS quota to enforce pod CPU limits. When the
> node runs many CPU-bound pods, the workload can move to different CPU cores
> depending on whether the pod is throttled and which CPU cores are available
> at scheduling time.

That is from [CPU Management
Policies](https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/cpu-management-policies/),
describing normal, correct behaviour. Getting exclusive cores back needs the
`static` policy, and even then only for containers that are "both part of a
`Guaranteed` pod and have integer CPU `requests`". The fix is node-level kubelet
configuration, and at the end of it you have a node dedicated to one pod with
pinned cores and a memory reservation: a private box with extra steps, under a
control plane that still believes it may evict you.

The rest of the mismatch:

- **The state is in RAM and it accumulates.** AzerothCore's memory page says the
  server caches world maps as players explore them and never unloads them until
  restart, ending at 11 GB or more, and recommends starting at 16 GB
  ([memory usage](https://www.azerothcore.org/wiki/es/memory-usage) — only the
  Spanish translation is live; the English one 404s). A pod restart is a server
  restart for every connected player.
- **There is no meaningful readiness semantics.** A world server that finishes
  loading is not interchangeable with the one it replaced. Rolling updates have
  nothing to roll to.
- **The connection is a long-lived raw TCP session**, not a request. Ingress
  controllers, retries and connection draining all assume otherwise.

## What does belong in the cluster

![The same system split: behind one load balancer, a cluster holding replicated auth daemons and the web front end over a shared database of accounts, characters and guilds; outside it, each world daemon on its own machine with its own read-mostly world database, reaching back into the shared database for characters and guilds.](/images/what-a-game-world-server-needs/split.svg)

Everything else, and it is more than you would guess. The auth daemon, whose
durable state lives in the database and whose process is close to stateless. The
website, the registration API, the account panel. Item shops, armory pages,
ladders and statistics — read-mostly and cacheable. Discord bots and status
pages. Backups, migrations and content-import pipelines, which is exactly what
Jobs and CronJobs are for. And the observability stack for all of it, including
the world box, which can push into the cluster without living in it.

That list is a normal small platform: GitOps delivery, per-tenant namespaces,
certificates, secrets, observability, reconciled from git rather than remembered.

The world box wants the opposite priorities, in an order a spec sheet does not
encourage. **Clock first**, for the reason above. **Then RAM, generously**,
because the map cache only grows — budget for a long uptime, not the first hour.
**Then local NVMe**, because the characters database takes writes at every save
interval and punishes a network volume. **Then network latency, not bandwidth**
— small packets, frequently, where distance and jitter decide how the game feels
and throughput never enters it.

## Where the line falls

Split the system by whether a process can be killed and replaced without anyone
noticing. Everything on the yes side — auth, web, shop, armory, stats, bots,
backups, jobs — is a normal workload, and a cluster buys it reconciled
configuration, generated isolation and one place to look when it breaks.

The world server is on the no side, alone: one long-lived process holding a
world in memory, advancing it on a clock, bounded by a single thread, talking
raw TCP to clients who will notice a restart within one second. It wants a fast
core, a lot of RAM, a local disk and a short path to its players. It wants,
almost exactly, [one box](/blog/athletos-freebsd/).

The mistake is not choosing Kubernetes. The mistake is assuming the whole system
has one shape. It has two, and the cheapest architecture is the one that admits
it.

``` =html
<p class="colophon">Everything above is read rather than run: TrinityCore's source and shipped configuration, the AzerothCore and CMaNGOS documentation, the Kubernetes docs and PassMark's published ratings. The threading claim is a design established from source, not a profile of a populated server.</p>
```
